 {"id": "AGENT-1", "title": "Give the agent a real agentic context window, real tool calls, and the ability to delegate research to a subagent", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 5, "depends_on": [], "blocks": null, "primary_model": null, "primary_provider": null, "fallback_model": null, "fallback_provider": null, "reasoning": "MEASURED GAP (operator question, 2026-09-26): 'is the agent getting turns and past events like a coding-agent context window, and is it wired to make tool calls so it can think, decide, and delegate research?' Answer from the code: NO, not yet. (1) CONTEXT: every cycle is a fresh single-turn request - controller_plan builds messages=[system, user] and nothing else (cron_runner.py ~1744). There is no message history and no persisted transcript anywhere. The past survives only as (a) _mem_notes capped at 6 entries and a single _mem_goal string (cron_runner.py 3091-3092, joined and truncated to 300 chars at 3577), (b) boot_memory injected at boot (MEM-2), and (c) an in-run `results` list handed to JEV/teacher as recent_events - an event log, not a context window. So the agent cannot see its own recent reasoning or re-read text it consumed. (2) TOOLS: src/core/ai_client.py DOES support native OpenAI function calling (tools param 562, payload wiring 595-599, tool_calls parsing 710-713, text-only model fallback 861-899) - but the controller path never uses it: the controller returns a JSON plan (a list of button presses). _battle_tool_call looks like tool use but is a FIXED translation table from JEV battle vocabulary (MOVE_n/RUN/SWITCH) to emulator transport calls - the model is not choosing tools. (3) DELEGATION: grep for subagent/delegate/research finds NOTHING. ACCEPTANCE: (a) each cycle carries a BOUNDED rolling context window (last N turns plus a compressed running summary) so the agent sees its own recent decisions, results, and the text it read; (b) the controller can emit REAL tool calls that the harness executes (walk/interact/read-dialogue/query-memory/look-up) instead of only emitting a button plan; (c) the agent can DELEGATE: spawn a bounded research subagent for an online lookup and receive a compressed finding back into its context and DuckBrain; (d) all of it cost-capped, bounded, and stamped per decision-mode so the pure-LLM benchmark (BENCH-1) stays comparable; (e) EVIDENCE = one run whose log shows at least one model-CHOSEN tool call and at least one delegated research result that is consumed in a LATER cycle.", "created_at": "2026-09-26 01:05:00"}
 {"id": "GUARD-1", "title": "The pre-commit guard FAILS OPEN on timeout: the repo suite (368s under load) exceeds the 300s hook timeout, so commits land with lint red while the guard prints PASS", "status": "complete", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 3, "depends_on": [], "blocks": null, "primary_model": null, "primary_provider": null, "fallback_model": null, "fallback_provider": null, "reasoning": "MEASURED 2026-09-26, same staged diff, two consecutive commit attempts, opposite verdicts. Attempt 1 (05:26): guard COMPLETED in 245.79s -> FAILED honestly -> 'lint: ruff format --check: 1 file(s) would be reformatted (tests/test_decision_mode.py)' + 'static_analysis: 12 mypy errors in scripts/long_run.py' -> commit REJECTED. Attempt 2 (05:28): same index, printed 'WARNING Guard timed out after 300s (hook_timeout). Remaining checks skipped - commit allowed to proceed (fail-open).' and 'Tier 1 Guards: PASS' -- IN THE SAME OUTPUT as 'X lint - 1 file would be reformatted' -- and the commit LANDED (61e96a2) and was pushed. So the SAME red lint both blocked and passed a commit depending only on machine load: the repo's own suite measured 368.51s (6:08) inside the guard while a 3-hour game run was consuming the box, vs a 300s internal hook timeout. A guard whose verdict is decided by ambient load is not a gate. Two compounding defects: (1) .gitreins/config.yaml sets allow_skips: true, so skipped checks do not fail the lane; (2) test_command is './venv/bin/pytest' which resolves to a STALE venv (venv/, mtime 2026-07-20) while the project's real venv is .venv/ (mtime 2026-09-23) -- both exist, so 'tests (full)' passing may not mean the CURRENT interpreter/dependency set passed. ACCEPTANCE: a guard run either completes inside its budget and gates honestly, or fails CLOSED with an explicit 'guard did not complete' non-pass verdict; a timeout must NEVER print PASS; the hook budget must exceed the measured worst-case suite time (or the hook must run a bounded subset and defer the full gate to CI). EVIDENCE: a deliberately-red staged file is REJECTED on a loaded box, and the same file produces a non-zero exit.", "created_at": "2026-09-26 01:20:00", "updated_at": "2026-09-26 10:20:00", "foreman_note": "T247: closed as residual bookkeeping - work landed at T246: 344242a fail-closed wrapper + config (allow_skips=false, hook_timeout=900, .venv test_command), 4743ce1 skip-policy refine, c5fe4c6 verdict fold. Tier1 5/5 full green 248s 0 skips THROUGH the new hook; tier2 verdict f0210092 PASS COMPLETE (task_id=GUARD-1, .gitreins/history/2026-09-26/f0210092). Lifecycle already complete - not re-judged per standing doctrine. Row's own acceptance (timeout never prints PASS; budget exceeds worst-case suite) satisfied.", "worker_summary": "344242a: scripts/hooks/pre-commit fail-closed wrapper (rejects guard-timeout text, requires fresh guard run log, passes real failures through, allows uninit repos); .gitreins/config.yaml allow_skips=false + hook_timeout=900 + test_command ./.venv/bin/pytest (stale-venv fix); pylsp added (requirements-dev + .venv); tests/test_precommit_hook.py 7 cases incl stale-verdict rejection.", "worker_status": "complete", "completed_at": "2026-09-26 10:20:00", "commit_hash": "344242a"}
 {"id": "SPEC-AGENTIC", "title": "Agentic memory loop spec (S1..S7: label, store, retrieve, delegate) — design authority for the agentic workstream", "status": "complete", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 2, "depends_on": [], "blocks": null, "primary_model": null, "primary_provider": null, "fallback_model": null, "fallback_provider": null, "reasoning": "DESIGN AUTHORITY for the agentic workstream; filed complete because docs/specs/SPEC_agentic_memory_loop.md exists in the tree (149 lines) and is committed with this workstream. Covers: (0) the measured audit that motivates it - every cycle is a fresh single-turn request (messages=[system,user], no history, no persisted transcript), _mem_notes is capped at 6 entries joined and truncated to 300 chars with a single _mem_goal string, src/core/ai_client.py supports native OpenAI function calling but the controller path never uses it, grep for subagent/delegate/research across cron_runner.py and src/core/ returns nothing, and the DuckBrain client (src/core/duckbrain_client.py, namespace pokemon-global) is used only for an end-of-run summary row and a boot-memory block; consequence in one number: map_topology is 1,219 of 1,352 escalations (90%). (1) Six principles. (2) The memory contract: namespaces pokemon-global and pokemon-run:<run_id>, the 8-label taxonomy (world/map/*, world/object/*, world/npc/*, world/path/*, world/dialog/*, world/mechanics/*, run/<run_id>/goal, run/<run_id>/failure/*), the per-write field set (key, domain, attributes, embedding_text, confidence, evidence, applies_when) and the three retrieval tools. (3) The bounded context-window contract (system, rolling window N=12, compressed summary, top-K=8 memory block, projection+screenshot; each with a char ceiling). (4) The 7-tool model-chosen surface. (5) The delegation contract (bounded subagent, capped finding, acceptance requires consumption in a LATER cycle). (6) Stages S1..S7 with their acceptance criteria and dependency order S1 -> S2 -> {S3,S4} -> S5 -> S6, S7 after S4. (7) Non-goals (no seeded story; not a JEV replacement; no unbounded transcript; benchmark meaning unchanged). Every other row in this workstream derives from this document; if code and the spec disagree, the spec wins until amended here.", "created_at": "2026-09-26 00:45:46"}
-{"id": "MEM-API", "title": "S1 memory API — a written fact is retrievable by key AND by label AND by similarity, after a process restart", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 4, "depends_on": ["SPEC-AGENTIC"], "blocks": null, "primary_model": null, "primary_provider": null, "fallback_model": null, "fallback_provider": null, "reasoning": "STAGE S1 of SPEC_agentic_memory_loop.md. ACCEPTANCE (verbatim from the spec): \"a written fact is retrievable by key AND by label AND by similarity, **after a process restart**\". Measured starting point from the spec's audit: the client src/core/duckbrain_client.py (namespace pokemon-global) already exposes remember / recall / get / list_keys / search, but the loop uses it only for an end-of-run summary row and a boot-memory block - the agent cannot label its own knowledge and pull it back on demand. The spec's retrieval surface is memory.get(key) exact, memory.query(labels=[...], limit=N) by label/domain, and memory.search(text, limit=N) by similarity, and retrieval must read the store rather than in-process state, which is what the restart clause pins down. ORDERED FIRST because every later stage reads memory: it is S1 in the spec's dependency chain S1 -> S2 -> {S3,S4} -> S5 -> S6, and S2's 'the NEXT cycle retrieves one' clause cannot be demonstrated before the read path exists.", "created_at": "2026-09-26 00:45:46"}
+{"id": "MEM-API", "title": "S1 memory API — a written fact is retrievable by key AND by label AND by similarity, after a process restart", "status": "in_progress", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 4, "depends_on": ["SPEC-AGENTIC"], "blocks": null, "primary_model": null, "primary_provider": null, "fallback_model": null, "fallback_provider": null, "reasoning": "STAGE S1 of SPEC_agentic_memory_loop.md. ACCEPTANCE (verbatim from the spec): \"a written fact is retrievable by key AND by label AND by similarity, **after a process restart**\". Measured starting point from the spec's audit: the client src/core/duckbrain_client.py (namespace pokemon-global) already exposes remember / recall / get / list_keys / search, but the loop uses it only for an end-of-run summary row and a boot-memory block - the agent cannot label its own knowledge and pull it back on demand. The spec's retrieval surface is memory.get(key) exact, memory.query(labels=[...], limit=N) by label/domain, and memory.search(text, limit=N) by similarity, and retrieval must read the store rather than in-process state, which is what the restart clause pins down. ORDERED FIRST because every later stage reads memory: it is S1 in the spec's dependency chain S1 -> S2 -> {S3,S4} -> S5 -> S6, and S2's 'the NEXT cycle retrieves one' clause cannot be demonstrated before the read path exists.", "created_at": "2026-09-26 00:45:46", "worktree": "/home/kara/worktrees/ai_plays_poke-MEM-API", "branch": "wt/MEM-API", "worker_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "worker_provider": "openai-codex"}
 {"id": "MEM-POP", "title": "S2 memory population — a 30-cycle run writes >=1 world/map/* and >=1 world/object/*, and the NEXT cycle retrieves one", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 4, "depends_on": ["MEM-API"], "blocks": null, "primary_model": null, "primary_provider": null, "fallback_model": null, "fallback_provider": null, "reasoning": "STAGE S2 of SPEC_agentic_memory_loop.md. ACCEPTANCE (verbatim from the spec): \"a 30-cycle run writes >=1 `world/map/*` and >=1 `world/object/*`; the NEXT cycle retrieves one\". This is the stage that attacks the spec's headline measurement: map_topology is 1,219 of 1,352 escalations (90%), because the agent cannot remember 'I have been in this room; the exit is south' and re-derives the same world fact every episode while spending a teacher call on it each time. Writes must carry the full per-write field set (key, domain, attributes, typed facts, embedding_text, confidence, evidence with run id/cycle/map/tile, applies_when where conditional) and must use the label taxonomy's world/map/<map_id> and world/object/<map_id>/<x>_<y> forms, so the run has to emit labelled facts, not a summary blob. ORDERED DIRECTLY AFTER S1 because population is only provable once the retrieval API exists - the second half of the criterion ('the NEXT cycle retrieves one') is a read-back test, so shipping writes before reads would leave the stage unverifiable.", "created_at": "2026-09-26 00:45:46"}
 {"id": "CTX-WIN", "title": "S3 context window — the request carries prior turns, and the agent answers about a cycle >N back from the summary", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 4, "depends_on": ["MEM-POP"], "blocks": null, "primary_model": null, "primary_provider": null, "fallback_model": null, "fallback_provider": null, "reasoning": "STAGE S3 of SPEC_agentic_memory_loop.md. ACCEPTANCE (verbatim from the spec): \"the log shows prior turns in the request, and the agent answers about a cycle >N back from the summary\". Measured starting point from the spec: every cycle is a fresh single-turn request (messages=[{\"role\":\"system\",...},{\"role\":\"user\",...}] and nothing else), there is no message history and no persisted transcript anywhere in the repo, and the past survives only as _mem_notes (capped at 6 entries, joined and truncated to 300 chars) and one _mem_goal string plus the in-run results list that JEV/teacher read as 'recent events' - an event log, not a window. The target contract (spec section 3) assembles each request from a system block, a rolling window of the last N=12 (decision, action, result) triples, a compressed summary of earlier turns rebuilt as the window slides, and a top-K=8 relevant-memory block for the current map/tile/screen, each with its own character ceiling so per-cycle input cost stays predictable and the run cannot silently grow. ORDERED AFTER S2 because the relevant-memory block is populated from the store that S2 proves gets written; the spec places S3 (with S4) immediately after S2 in the chain S1 -> S2 -> {S3,S4} -> S5 -> S6.", "created_at": "2026-09-26 00:45:46"}
 {"id": "TOOLS-1", "title": "S4 tool calling — the log shows >=1 model-chosen tool call with its result, and a failed call is visible to the model", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 5, "depends_on": ["MEM-POP"], "blocks": null, "primary_model": null, "primary_provider": null, "fallback_model": null, "fallback_provider": null, "reasoning": "STAGE S4 of SPEC_agentic_memory_loop.md. ACCEPTANCE (verbatim from the spec): \"the log shows >=1 **model-chosen** tool call with its result; a failed call is visible to the model\". Measured starting point from the spec: src/core/ai_client.py does support native OpenAI function calling (tools param, tool_calls parsing, text-only-model fallback), but the controller path never uses it - it returns a JSON plan, a list of button presses - and _battle_tool_call only looks like tool use: it is a fixed translation table from JEV battle vocabulary (MOVE_n / RUN / SWITCH) to emulator transport calls, so the model is not choosing tools. Target surface (spec section 4): walk(direction, tiles), interact(target), read_dialog(), remember(key, domain, facts, applies_when), recall(key|labels|query), delegate_research(question, budget), set_goal(text, reason) / check_goal(). Rules that make the criterion checkable: the harness executes with verification, every call and its result is a decision row so the autonomy counters count tool use honestly, and a failed call surfaces its error to the model instead of silently degrading to a blind A-press. ORDERED AFTER S2 (not parallel with CTX-WIN) because two of the seven tools are remember/recall, which need the populated store and retrieval API to mean anything; S5 depends on this row because delegate_research is itself one of these model-chosen tools.", "created_at": "2026-09-26 00:45:46"}
